WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:05.410
The heisenberg uncertainty principles has
to be one of the coolest and also one of the

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:09.610
most misunderstood and abused ideas in physics.

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:15.309
So before I explain it you, I want you to
pick which of these 3 statements you think

00:00:15.309 --> 00:00:17.630
is the uncertainty principle.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:22.370
This isn’t to embarrass you, it’s because
it really helps to know where your preconceived

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:27.400
understanding is flawed to be able to fix
it so please please do it, it’s for your

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.900
own good.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.439
Even if you don’t know the principle or
none of these seem perfect, just pick the

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:37.040
one that sounds most right to you, and in
the corner of the video you can find a poll

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.280
where you can enter your guess.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.420
Please pause the video now to read these and
decide.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.320
All right, I hope you’ve made your choice.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.360
Maybe you’ll be surprised, but both 1 and
2 are very flawed.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.590
They demonstrate quite fundamental misconceptions
about quantum mechanics.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:01:01.980
But worse, they make it sound like the heisenberg
is about measuring- here’s the thing, it’s

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.440
not about measurement.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.610
The principle works whether or not we ever
measure the particle.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:13.840
Before I explain that, let me try convince
you the other two options are just not right.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.170
Option 1 first.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:20.810
The really big issue with this option is it
says quantum particles really have a position

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:25.920
and momentum, just like in classical physics;
but they’re just shy about it.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.930
This is not at all what quantum mechanics
says.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:34.030
What quantum says is, a particle doesn’t
just have one place or speed they’re going

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:35.299
at, instead.

00:01:35.299 --> 00:01:40.079
Instead, while they’re not observed, they
can be in what we call a superposition of

00:01:40.079 --> 00:01:42.729
many places, and many speeds.

00:01:42.729 --> 00:01:45.360
But, say you go to measure it.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.200
You can only measure one thing about the particle,
so let’s say you’re measuring the position.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.770
You’re not going to see it in all of these
places.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:58.270
It will just be in one- and it will pick which
one, randomly.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:02.369
So that’s one reason the first answer is
wrong.

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:06.420
When you measure a particle, it doesn’t
tell you a range of places or any other information

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.789
about where it is, it just turns up in one
single place.

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:14.430
And what’s worse is, you can’t measure
it again and hope to see one of those other

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:15.440
spots.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:21.350
Now it’s not in a superposition anymore,
if you measure it again, it will certainly

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.470
be in this place.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:29.060
In the language of quantum mechanics, a measurement
collapses the state- so the particle is no

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.870
longer doing what it was before.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:36.520
So it’s pretty meaningless to now measure
the speed to try and deduce anything about

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.959
the particle’s speed before you measured
anything.

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:41.500
No, you’ve ruined the original state.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.890
I mean you can measure the speed, and just
like with position, it will tell you just

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:52.410
one speed, but that is in no way the speed
the particle was really going or even related

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.790
to the superposition of speeds of the particle
originally.

00:02:55.790 --> 00:03:01.930
So in summary, the particle didn’t have
just one speed orposition.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:07.580
But if you measured say it’s position, it
would only tell you one of places, and which

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.400
one is random.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.670
Plus you couldn’t ask it again to get more
information, because it’s no longer in a

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:15.690
superposition of places.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:19.160
And worse, you can’t even ask the same particle
about its momentum now.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:24.830
The value you get is unrelated to the original
superposition of momentum.

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:30.310
On the other hand this option says the particles
really have one speed and one position.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:35.519
And That you can get some range of information
about the particles position, and that you

00:03:35.519 --> 00:03:39.599
can then also go ask about momentum too.

00:03:39.599 --> 00:03:41.500
What about the second option.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:46.190
This has got to be one of the most popular
explanations of the uncertainty principle

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:52.100
and it’s just so confusing, because it’s
completely unrelated the uncertainty principle.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:56.390
The principle is not about some very specific
set up for measuring position.

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:00.860
Implying that it is leads people to ask the
very sensible question, ‘well why don’t

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:03.760
we just come up with a better device?’

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.990
The heisenberg uncertainty principle is not
about measurement at all.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:12.280
I’ll explain in a sec, but first let’s
address the issues of this one.

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:16.560
It has some pretty similar misconceptions;
that the particle really has one position

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:21.900
and momentum and that we’re trying to outwit
the particle to find them both out.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:27.580
It also seems to say that the ‘uncertainty’
is from knocking the particle or some other

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:31.940
way of disturbing the particle via measurement.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:34.759
The uncertainty has nothing to do with measurement.

00:04:34.759 --> 00:04:39.290
It’s about the range of the different speeds
in the superposition.

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:43.660
This range is the ‘uncertainty’ referred
by the principle.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:48.160
But uncertainty is an unfortunate term for
it.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.970
Uncertainty makes people think there is a
speed, but we’re uncertain about what it

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:53.160
is.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:58.330
But no, quantum mechanics is so much weirder
and more exciting than that than that, I mean

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.760
this second option is basically classical.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:04.930
There’s pretty much nothing there that you
won’t expect from real life.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:07.949
Quantum mechanics is not what you’d expect
from everyday life.

00:05:07.949 --> 00:05:11.889
So let me explain the real heisenberg uncertainty
principle

00:05:11.889 --> 00:05:16.759
As I was saying before, the particle is in
a superposition of many places, but usually

00:05:16.759 --> 00:05:20.090
it’s not equally in all of the places.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:24.850
Some places are weighted more heavily, which
means that if you were to measure it, it’s

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:27.250
more likely to turn up in one of these places.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:29.050
But we’re not going to measure it.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:30.050
Ok?

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:32.289
There’s no measurement in this entire thing.

00:05:32.289 --> 00:05:33.289
Just to make that clear.

00:05:33.289 --> 00:05:37.240
Now let’s say a particle is most likely
to turn up in a certain areas.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:42.140
There’s a way to mathematically describe
the total size of these likely areas.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:44.669
We’ll use the symbol delta x.

00:05:44.669 --> 00:05:51.130
So a particle could have a really small delta
x, which means that it’s mostly in a superposition

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:53.389
over a small area.

00:05:53.389 --> 00:05:59.210
Which again means, if we were to measure where
it is, we could make a pretty good guess about

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:01.300
where the particle will turn up.

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:06.600
This is probably why the term uncertainty
is used to describe delta x, because it measures

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:11.520
how much uncertainty we’d have about the
result if we were to measure.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:16.680
I just don’t like to use that word because
of the connotation that it really was in one

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:19.830
place, but we didn’t have enough knowledge
to know where.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:20.830
No.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:25.380
Even with all the knowledge that quantum mechanics
says is possible for anyone, there’s still

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.919
this randomness when you measure.

00:06:27.919 --> 00:06:31.490
Ok, we can similarly talk about the momentum.

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:34.550
The particle is in this superposition of speeds.

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:37.210
So which speeds are weighted the most heavily?

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:43.180
Again, you’ll find it’s some range, and
you quantify the size of that range mathematically

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:46.639
the same way and call it delta p.

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:51.660
Now we have the stage set for understanding
that famous misused equation.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:56.810
What it says is, in quantum mechanics delta
x and delta p can’t both be as small as

00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:57.810
you want.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:03.680
See, if delta x was really really really really
small, but so was delta p, their product would

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:09.460
be very small, but that’s no good- it has
to be bigger than this number.

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:14.910
This is significant because while it’s true
that in quantum we know the particle is in

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:19.380
a superposition, we might have wanted to minimise
how bad that is.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:23.560
We might have thought, well at least if the
particle won’t just go at one speed, we

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:29.110
could come up with a way to really really
narrow down the range of speeds in the superposition.

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:35.580
And then, even if we have to accept the particle
is in a superposition of many places, at least

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:39.190
we can make it mostly in one small area.

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:45.920
Then this particle has almost one speed and
almost one position at a time is almost a

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:47.699
classical particle.

00:07:47.699 --> 00:07:51.400
So it will almost act like we want it to.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:54.790
But the heisenberg uncertainty principle destroys
that possibility.

00:07:54.790 --> 00:08:00.010
There’s no way to make a particle almost
classical in position and almost classical

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:01.610
in momentum.

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:06.889
One or both have to give, and the world is
quantum, not classical, no matter what we

00:08:06.889 --> 00:08:08.940
wish.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:12.930
If you’re new here and you’ve made it
to this point, I’m honestly impressed.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:18.419
I’m sure plenty of the points that seemed
really odd or were poorly explained.

00:08:18.419 --> 00:08:23.360
But if you’re interested in knowing the
details then you can start here.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:28.349
For everyone else, you get a small break from
homework just this time because I want to

00:08:28.349 --> 00:08:35.330
make a video soon that talks about how the
heisenberg uncertainty comes about naturally

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:53.579
from all the things we’ve already talk about,
and I want to get onto that video very quickly.

